From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: vfe-17x: Add the display output of the pixel path

The Titan 175 IFE has a second processed image output beside FULL, the
display path: an MNDS16 of its own (luma 0x1040, chroma 0x106c, laid out
like the FULL ones), crop and round/clamp (0x1134..0x1158), enabled by
bits 3 and 4 of 0xfb0, written by write masters 20 (Y) and 21 (CbCr). It
takes the frame after CST at the CAMIF size, like FULL, in the same NV12
or NV21 order, so one sensor frame comes out in two sizes, for instance
a preview beside a full size still.

Give each full VFE with a pixel pipeline a display output: a subdev
msm_vfeN_pix_disp and a video node msm_vfeN_video_disp, with the formats
of the pixel line's video node. Links:

  msm_vfeN_pix:1 -> msm_vfeN_pix_disp:0     (created off)
  msm_vfeN_pix_disp:1 -> msm_vfeN_video_disp (immutable)

The sink pad takes the frame the display path scales (the CAMIF frame;
with dual IFE the whole frame of both stripes). The sink compose
rectangle is the scaler output: even, no larger than the sink and at
most 16 times smaller per axis; the source crop is the part of it
written, the size of the source pad and the buffers. The FULL node
starts the pixel path; the display node starts only its subdev, and
needs a running pixel path, the link on and the formats matching.

With the display link on, the display output is required: a frame is
written only when the FULL and the display output can both take a
buffer for it, and both are armed for it together, so the two images of
a request come from one frame. Frames are numbered from the first one
written, for all buffers and the frame start events.

A request may leave out the FULL or the RDI buffer. The parameters
buffer comes one per written frame, taken in order, so let it name the
frame's outputs: the block IFE17X_PARAMS_BLOCK_OUTPUTS lists FULL,
DISPLAY and RDI, at least one image output. The next frame is decided
once, from the parameters buffer at the head of the queue; it is written
once every named output can take a buffer, and exactly those get one.
Without the block the FULL output decides as before. Whether an output
has a buffer for the next frame is tracked (armed_next, armed_ahead)
instead of guessed from the buffers out.

With dual IFE the master's display output stands for the whole frame.
The MNDS stripe registers do not behave as documented, so both display
scalers start at phase 0 at their first column and step by the frame's
ratio; the slave's stripe starts at the even column nearest its input
offset over the step, and the seam is the column nearest the split
whose offset in the buffer is a multiple of 16 bytes (or 8, 4, 2).
Userspace chooses the split to keep the residual small (about 0.1
display pixel at the common sizes). A display buffer completes once
both display composite groups have, paired by time.

The block IFE17X_PARAMS_BLOCK_DISPLAY_WINDOW sets the scaler output and
the crop position per frame, for digital zoom on the display output; the
output size stays fixed. The window is programmed when its frame is
settled and latches with the frame's register update; with dual IFE the
stripes are recomputed for it, and a window within one stripe's reach is
written by that IFE alone. The write masters' widths latch at the frame
boundary.

A corrupt frame of the pixel line, from a VFE error interrupt or a CSID
receiver error, fails the display buffer of that frame too.

A write master disabled with an address queued keeps it for the next
start, so a display stop gives it no more buffers, waits until the
frames it has addresses for are written, then turns the path off. The
pixel path stop does the same first; a display start is refused while
the pixel path stops. The camss stop walk changes to fit: the subdev
that feeds the node stops with it; a subdev that directly feeds another
streaming video node counts as shared.

